WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.720
<v Tutor>Welcome to Part Two Daily.

2
00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:05.412
<v Tutor>It's Wednesday the eleventh of November, and this is a deep dive.

3
00:00:05.412 --> 00:00:08.802
<v Tutor>Today we stay with one problem until it gives up its marks.

4
00:00:08.802 --> 00:00:11.432
<v Tutor>The problem is a filter that keeps clotting.

5
00:00:11.432 --> 00:00:13.557
<v Tutor>It sounds like a nursing headache.

6
00:00:13.557 --> 00:00:21.498
<v Tutor>In the exam it is a renal question, a haematology question and a pharmacology question at once, and in 2014 fewer than half the candidates passed it.

7
00:00:21.498 --> 00:00:23.167
<v Tutor>Here's the running order.

8
00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:30.502
<v Tutor>First, a tutorial on keeping the continuous renal replacement circuit alive, including the patient with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.

9
00:00:30.502 --> 00:00:35.813
<v Tutor>Second, the two trials that made citrate the default, one of them from Australia and New Zealand.

10
00:00:35.813 --> 00:00:41.074
<v Tutor>Third, exam practice on a real College question about citrate, with an examiner and a candidate.

11
00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:42.440
<v Tutor>Then a short close.

12
00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.568
<v Tutor>Picture three in the morning.

13
00:00:45.568 --> 00:00:48.790
<v Tutor>Your patient is on continuous veno-venous haemodiafiltration.

14
00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:51.031
<v Tutor>The third filter today has just clotted.

15
00:00:51.031 --> 00:00:53.226
<v Tutor>The nurse shows you the platelet count.

16
00:00:53.226 --> 00:00:54.720
<v Tutor>It was 240 on admission.

17
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:55.794
<v Tutor>Today it is 70.

18
00:00:55.794 --> 00:00:59.716
<v Tutor>The patient has been on heparin for seven days, and the left leg is swollen.

19
00:00:59.716 --> 00:01:01.584
<v Tutor>What do you do with the circuit?

20
00:01:01.584 --> 00:01:02.798
<v Tutor>Hold that thought.

21
00:01:02.798 --> 00:01:06.160
<v Tutor>By the end of this part you should have a ten-mark answer ready.

22
00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.087
<v Tutor>First, how the College asks this.

23
00:01:09.087 --> 00:01:14.325
<v Tutor>In the curriculum data, the circuit has been examined seven times since 2007.

24
00:01:14.325 --> 00:01:23.877
<v Tutor>Three questions ask for strategies to prolong the life of the circuit: the 2007 first sitting, the 2010 second sitting, and the 2014 first sitting.

25
00:01:23.877 --> 00:01:30.163
<v Tutor>Two ask about citrate anticoagulation specifically, in 2013 and again, word for word, in 2015.

26
00:01:30.163 --> 00:01:34.970
<v Tutor>Two more ask you to troubleshoot a dialysis circuit, in 2009 and 2018.

27
00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.188
<v Tutor>The pass rates tell a story.

28
00:01:37.188 --> 00:01:40.948
<v Tutor>The 2007 and 2010 versions were passed by 71 percent.

29
00:01:40.948 --> 00:01:45.878
<v Tutor>The 2014 version, which is on your list today, was passed by 43 percent.

30
00:01:45.878 --> 00:01:49.699
<v Tutor>The citrate question in 2015 was passed by 86 percent.

31
00:01:49.699 --> 00:01:52.472
<v Tutor>So candidates know citrate chemistry.

32
00:01:52.472 --> 00:02:00.360
<v Tutor>What they lose marks on is the whole-patient strategy, and especially the patient with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.

33
00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:04.800
<v Tutor>Here is the 2014 first sitting, question 29, read exactly.

34
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:15.758
<v Tutor>A 55-year-old patient with severe sepsis develops Heparin Induced Thrombotic Thrombocytopaenia Syndrome, HITTS, while on continuous veno-venous haemodiafiltration, CVVHDF.

35
00:02:15.758 --> 00:02:25.858
<v Tutor>Outline the strategies available for prolonging the life of the CVVHDF circuit in this patient, mentioning the advantages and disadvantages of each strategy.

36
00:02:25.858 --> 00:02:27.756
<v Tutor>Notice the instruction.

37
00:02:27.756 --> 00:02:32.041
<v Tutor>Outline, with advantages and disadvantages, for each strategy.

38
00:02:32.041 --> 00:02:33.510
<v Tutor>That is a table.

39
00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:41.223
<v Tutor>The College's own discussion of the near-identical 2010 question said the answer would have benefited from tabulation.

40
00:02:41.223 --> 00:02:44.834
<v Tutor>Three columns: strategy, advantages, disadvantages.

41
00:02:44.834 --> 00:02:46.426
<v Tutor>Six to eight rows.

42
00:02:46.426 --> 00:02:50.160
<v Tutor>Write it as a table and you will not forget a column.

43
00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:51.813
<v Tutor>Now the framework.

44
00:02:51.813 --> 00:02:53.886
<v Tutor>Think of filter life in two layers.

45
00:02:53.886 --> 00:02:56.633
<v Tutor>The first layer is everything that is not a drug.

46
00:02:56.633 --> 00:02:58.850
<v Tutor>The second layer is the anticoagulant.

47
00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:06.320
<v Tutor>Examiners reward candidates who start with the first layer, because it is where the free marks are and because it is what a consultant fixes first.

48
00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.033
<v Tutor>Before any drug: name three things that determine how long a filter lasts.

49
00:03:11.033 --> 00:03:12.400
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

50
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:19.309
<v Tutor>Here is the answer.

51
00:03:19.309 --> 00:03:20.539
<v Tutor>First, access.

52
00:03:20.539 --> 00:03:23.782
<v Tutor>Second, flow and haemoconcentration in the filter.

53
00:03:23.782 --> 00:03:25.403
<v Tutor>Third, interruptions.

54
00:03:25.403 --> 00:03:27.080
<v Tutor>Let's go through each.

55
00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:28.644
<v Tutor>Access first.

56
00:03:28.644 --> 00:03:32.027
<v Tutor>Most early clotting is an access problem in disguise.

57
00:03:32.027 --> 00:03:35.686
<v Tutor>A poorly flowing catheter causes repeated pressure alarms.

58
00:03:35.686 --> 00:03:37.959
<v Tutor>Every alarm stops the blood pump.

59
00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:39.567
<v Tutor>Stagnant blood clots.

60
00:03:39.567 --> 00:03:47.052
<v Tutor>So you want a wide-bore, double-lumen dialysis catheter, of the right length for the site, with its tip where flow is generous.

61
00:03:47.052 --> 00:03:57.975
<v Tutor>The 2012 Kidney Disease Improving Global Outcomes guideline, known as KDIGO, ranks the sites: right internal jugular first, femoral second, left internal jugular third, and subclavian last.

62
00:03:57.975 --> 00:04:02.522
<v Tutor>The subclavian is last because of stenosis that can ruin a future fistula.

63
00:04:02.522 --> 00:04:07.401
<v Tutor>Kinking, patient position and a catheter tip against the vessel wall all matter.

64
00:04:07.401 --> 00:04:14.720
<v Tutor>If the access pressure swings every time the patient coughs or sits up, fix the catheter before you blame the anticoagulant.

65
00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.516
<v Tutor>Second, flow and haemoconcentration.

66
00:04:17.516 --> 00:04:20.239
<v Tutor>Blood in a haemofilter loses plasma water.

67
00:04:20.239 --> 00:04:25.140
<v Tutor>The further you concentrate it, the thicker it gets and the more readily it clots.

68
00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:31.021
<v Tutor>The measure is the filtration fraction: the proportion of plasma flow you take off as ultrafiltrate.

69
00:04:31.021 --> 00:04:35.323
<v Tutor>In post-dilution haemofiltration, keep it under about 20 to 25 percent.

70
00:04:35.323 --> 00:04:37.283
<v Tutor>You can lower it three ways.

71
00:04:37.283 --> 00:04:41.477
<v Tutor>Raise the blood flow, typically to 150 to 250 millilitres per minute.

72
00:04:41.477 --> 00:04:46.432
<v Tutor>Give some or all of the replacement fluid before the filter, which is pre-dilution.

73
00:04:46.432 --> 00:04:52.204
<v Tutor>Or shift clearance from convection to diffusion, by using dialysate rather than replacement fluid.

74
00:04:52.204 --> 00:04:53.947
<v Tutor>Pre-dilution is a trade.

75
00:04:53.947 --> 00:05:03.640
<v Tutor>It lengthens filter life, but it dilutes the blood you are trying to clean, so you lose some clearance and must prescribe a little more effluent to deliver the same dose.

76
00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.739
<v Tutor>Third, interruptions.

77
00:05:05.739 --> 00:05:08.025
<v Tutor>Every stop is a chance to clot.

78
00:05:08.025 --> 00:05:14.412
<v Tutor>Prompt response to alarms, trained nursing staff, and planned circuit changes all extend filter life.

79
00:05:14.412 --> 00:05:19.334
<v Tutor>The venous bubble trap, where blood meets air, is a favourite site for clot.

80
00:05:19.334 --> 00:05:21.033
<v Tutor>And watch the trends.

81
00:05:21.033 --> 00:05:28.592
<v Tutor>A rising pre-filter pressure and a rising transmembrane pressure tell you the filter is failing before it actually clots.

82
00:05:28.592 --> 00:05:35.800
<v Tutor>A falling ratio of effluent urea to blood urea tells you the membrane is fouling and clearance is quietly dropping.

83
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:39.592
<v Tutor>The 2018 first sitting turned this into a data question.

84
00:05:39.592 --> 00:05:43.089
<v Tutor>You were given circuit pressures and asked what was wrong.

85
00:05:43.089 --> 00:05:45.845
<v Tutor>So learn the pattern of the three pressures.

86
00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:48.918
<v Tutor>The access pressure, before the pump, is negative.

87
00:05:48.918 --> 00:05:52.575
<v Tutor>The pre-filter pressure sits between the pump and the filter.

88
00:05:52.575 --> 00:05:56.178
<v Tutor>The return pressure is on the venous side, after the filter.

89
00:05:56.178 --> 00:06:06.565
<v Tutor>If the access pressure becomes much more negative, the problem is drawing blood out: a kinked or malpositioned catheter, the tip sucking against the vessel wall, or a hypovolaemic patient.

90
00:06:06.565 --> 00:06:13.560
<v Tutor>If the return pressure rises, the problem is getting blood back: a clot in the venous chamber or the return limb, or a kink.

91
00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:20.078
<v Tutor>If the pre-filter pressure climbs while the return pressure stays the same or falls, the filter itself is clotting.

92
00:06:20.078 --> 00:06:22.781
<v Tutor>The pressure drop across the filter widens.

93
00:06:22.781 --> 00:06:29.193
<v Tutor>The transmembrane pressure rises as the membrane clogs with protein, which is called fouling, and then with clot.

94
00:06:29.193 --> 00:06:31.260
<v Tutor>That is a filter about to fail.

95
00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:38.520
<v Tutor>Change it electively before it clots with a full blood volume inside, which can be 150 to 200 millilitres of the patient's blood.

96
00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:46.829
<v Tutor>A consultant habit worth stating in an answer: when filter life suddenly shortens in a patient whose regimen hasn't changed, look for a new cause.

97
00:06:46.829 --> 00:06:48.678
<v Tutor>The catheter may have moved.

98
00:06:48.678 --> 00:06:51.143
<v Tutor>The patient may be more volume depleted.

99
00:06:51.143 --> 00:06:56.896
<v Tutor>Or something new may be driving coagulation, such as sepsis, or heparin-induced thrombocytopenia itself.

100
00:06:56.896 --> 00:07:04.240
<v Tutor>Recurrent unexplained clotting on heparin, with a falling platelet count, is one of the classic ways HIT first shows itself in the ICU.

101
00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:06.987
<v Tutor>Why does this matter beyond the circuit?

102
00:07:06.987 --> 00:07:08.844
<v Tutor>Because downtime is lost dose.

103
00:07:08.844 --> 00:07:15.151
<v Tutor>If you prescribe 25 millilitres per kilogram per hour and the filter is down a quarter of the day, you deliver much less.

104
00:07:15.151 --> 00:07:19.698
<v Tutor>That is one reason the big dose trials prescribed more than they intended to deliver.

105
00:07:19.698 --> 00:07:21.947
<v Tutor>And downtime means lost fluid removal.

106
00:07:21.947 --> 00:07:26.640
<v Tutor>A circuit that clots four times a day cannot de-resuscitate a patient in fluid overload.

107
00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:29.567
<v Tutor>Now the second layer, the anticoagulant.

108
00:07:29.567 --> 00:07:34.253
<v Tutor>Let me tell you where the most familiar one came from, because it explains a lot.

109
00:07:34.253 --> 00:07:40.413
<v Tutor>In 1916, Jay McLean was a medical student at Johns Hopkins working in William Howell's physiology laboratory.

110
00:07:40.413 --> 00:07:44.941
<v Tutor>Howell believed that a substance called cephalin was the body's clot promoter.

111
00:07:44.941 --> 00:07:48.837
<v Tutor>McLean's job was to purify it and prove that it helped blood clot.

112
00:07:48.837 --> 00:07:51.575
<v Tutor>He tried extracts from heart and from liver.

113
00:07:51.575 --> 00:07:53.838
<v Tutor>The liver extract did the opposite.

114
00:07:53.838 --> 00:07:55.629
<v Tutor>It stopped blood clotting.

115
00:07:55.629 --> 00:07:57.787
<v Tutor>McLean called it heparphosphatid.

116
00:07:57.787 --> 00:08:03.421
<v Tutor>Howell and his colleague Emmett Holt introduced the name heparin in 1918, from the Greek for liver.

117
00:08:03.421 --> 00:08:12.108
<v Tutor>It took the Toronto group under Charles Best, in the 1930s, to purify it well enough for humans, and Gordon Murray's clinical studies were published in 1937.

118
00:08:12.108 --> 00:08:17.320
<v Tutor>So the drug we put in every circuit began as a failed experiment to find the opposite drug.

119
00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:20.772
<v Tutor>And it took decades to see heparin's paradox.

120
00:08:20.772 --> 00:08:26.761
<v Tutor>In 1958, Weismann and Tobin described arterial emboli occurring during systemic heparin therapy.

121
00:08:26.761 --> 00:08:35.457
<v Tutor>In 1977, Rhodes, Dixon and Silver described eight patients with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia and thrombotic and haemorrhagic complications.

122
00:08:35.457 --> 00:08:37.414
<v Tutor>We now know the mechanism.

123
00:08:37.414 --> 00:08:41.906
<v Tutor>Antibodies form against complexes of heparin and platelet factor four.

124
00:08:41.906 --> 00:08:44.786
<v Tutor>Those immune complexes activate platelets.

125
00:08:44.786 --> 00:08:48.010
<v Tutor>The platelet count falls, and the patient clots.

126
00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:50.602
<v Tutor>That is the trap in today's question.

127
00:08:50.602 --> 00:08:54.172
<v Tutor>A patient with HITTS does not have a bleeding problem.

128
00:08:54.172 --> 00:08:57.800
<v Tutor>They have a clotting problem with a low platelet count.

129
00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.063
<v Tutor>So, the anticoagulant options.

130
00:09:00.063 --> 00:09:06.240
<v Tutor>I'll give you each one in the way the College answer lays them out: what it is, its advantages, and its disadvantages.

131
00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:12.236
<v Tutor>Option one: unfractionated heparin, given systemically, usually as a pre-filter infusion.

132
00:09:12.236 --> 00:09:18.121
<v Tutor>In the RICH trial the target was an activated partial thromboplastin time of 45 to 60 seconds.

133
00:09:18.121 --> 00:09:22.910
<v Tutor>Advantages: cheap, familiar, easy to monitor and reversible with protamine.

134
00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:26.949
<v Tutor>Disadvantages: bleeding, and heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.

135
00:09:26.949 --> 00:09:30.295
<v Tutor>In this patient, it is absolutely contraindicated.

136
00:09:30.295 --> 00:09:32.719
<v Tutor>Every source of heparin must stop.

137
00:09:32.719 --> 00:09:39.296
<v Tutor>That includes the heparin flushes on arterial lines, heparin catheter locks, and heparin-bonded membranes.

138
00:09:39.296 --> 00:09:41.488
<v Tutor>Say that sentence in the exam.

139
00:09:41.488 --> 00:09:44.200
<v Tutor>It is a mark that most candidates miss.

140
00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:51.194
<v Tutor>Two variations on heparin appear in the College answer to the 2007 question, so know them for a patient without HIT.

141
00:09:51.194 --> 00:09:58.533
<v Tutor>Regional heparin means heparin before the filter and protamine after it, to neutralise it before the blood returns to the patient.

142
00:09:58.533 --> 00:10:01.458
<v Tutor>The advantage is less systemic anticoagulation.

143
00:10:01.458 --> 00:10:13.371
<v Tutor>The disadvantages are that the ratio is hard to get right, protamine has its own risks, including hypotension and anaphylaxis, and the heparin protamine complex can dissociate later and cause rebound anticoagulation.

144
00:10:13.371 --> 00:10:18.210
<v Tutor>KDIGO suggests avoiding regional heparin in patients at increased risk of bleeding.

145
00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:21.401
<v Tutor>Low molecular weight heparin is the other variation.

146
00:10:21.401 --> 00:10:23.741
<v Tutor>Its advantage is predictable dosing.

147
00:10:23.741 --> 00:10:35.175
<v Tutor>Its disadvantages in this setting are renal accumulation, monitoring that needs anti-ten-A levels, only partial reversal by protamine, and a higher risk of HIT antibodies cross-reacting than with danaparoid.

148
00:10:35.175 --> 00:10:40.440
<v Tutor>In a patient with HIT, both variations are contraindicated, because they are still heparin.

149
00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:44.742
<v Tutor>Option two: no anticoagulant, with or without saline flushes.

150
00:10:44.742 --> 00:10:47.795
<v Tutor>Advantages: no bleeding risk from the circuit.

151
00:10:47.795 --> 00:10:51.414
<v Tutor>Disadvantages: short filter life and more interruptions.

152
00:10:51.414 --> 00:10:55.033
<v Tutor>Saline flushes add volume and their benefit is doubtful.

153
00:10:55.033 --> 00:10:57.747
<v Tutor>And here is the key point for this stem.

154
00:10:57.747 --> 00:11:04.363
<v Tutor>In HITTS, no anticoagulation is not an option for the patient, even if it might be an option for the circuit.

155
00:11:04.363 --> 00:11:09.000
<v Tutor>The patient has a thrombosis and needs a non-heparin anticoagulant anyway.

156
00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:11.647
<v Tutor>Option three: regional citrate.

157
00:11:11.647 --> 00:11:15.218
<v Tutor>We'll do the chemistry in detail in the exam practice.

158
00:11:15.218 --> 00:11:22.533
<v Tutor>In short, citrate binds ionised calcium in the circuit, and calcium is needed at several steps of the clotting cascade.

159
00:11:22.533 --> 00:11:30.022
<v Tutor>You infuse citrate before the filter, aiming for a post-filter ionised calcium of about 0.25 to 0.35 millimoles per litre.

160
00:11:30.022 --> 00:11:35.149
<v Tutor>You replace calcium into the patient to keep the systemic ionised calcium normal.

161
00:11:35.149 --> 00:11:41.715
<v Tutor>Advantages: the longest filter life, and the least bleeding, because anticoagulation stays in the circuit.

162
00:11:41.715 --> 00:11:48.167
<v Tutor>Disadvantages: complexity, protocols and frequent monitoring of sodium, ionised calcium and bicarbonate.

163
00:11:48.167 --> 00:11:52.487
<v Tutor>It can cause metabolic alkalosis, hypocalcaemia and hypernatraemia.

164
00:11:52.487 --> 00:11:57.383
<v Tutor>It is risky in liver failure and low-output shock, where citrate accumulates.

165
00:11:57.383 --> 00:12:03.144
<v Tutor>And in HITTS, citrate looks after the circuit but does nothing for the patient's thrombosis.

166
00:12:03.144 --> 00:12:06.600
<v Tutor>You still need a systemic non-heparin anticoagulant.

167
00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:10.920
<v Tutor>That sentence is in the College's discussion of this very question.

168
00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:17.173
<v Tutor>Let me add the practical citrate management the examiners expect, because the College asks it in detail.

169
00:12:17.173 --> 00:12:20.047
<v Tutor>Citrate delivery is proportional to blood flow.

170
00:12:20.047 --> 00:12:23.235
<v Tutor>Turn the blood pump up, and you deliver more citrate.

171
00:12:23.235 --> 00:12:36.561
<v Tutor>So if a patient on citrate develops a metabolic alkalosis, the usual moves are to lower the blood flow, which lowers the citrate load, or to increase the dialysate or effluent flow, which removes more calcium citrate before it reaches the patient.

172
00:12:36.561 --> 00:12:39.905
<v Tutor>Some units also switch to a lower-bicarbonate dialysate.

173
00:12:39.905 --> 00:12:44.504
<v Tutor>If the sodium is creeping up, the cause is usually hypertonic trisodium citrate.

174
00:12:44.504 --> 00:12:46.960
<v Tutor>Check the protocol's fluid composition.

175
00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:48.833
<v Tutor>You're on citrate.

176
00:12:48.833 --> 00:12:55.573
<v Tutor>The systemic ionised calcium has fallen to 0.9, and your calcium infusion has been increased three times today.

177
00:12:55.573 --> 00:12:58.858
<v Tutor>The total calcium is now 3.1 millimoles per litre.

178
00:12:58.858 --> 00:13:01.407
<v Tutor>What's happening, and what do you do?

179
00:13:01.407 --> 00:13:02.880
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

180
00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:09.727
<v Tutor>Divide 3.1 by 0.9.

181
00:13:09.727 --> 00:13:12.342
<v Tutor>The ratio is about 3.4, well above 2.5.

182
00:13:12.342 --> 00:13:14.123
<v Tutor>Citrate is accumulating.

183
00:13:14.123 --> 00:13:16.127
<v Tutor>The liver isn't clearing it.

184
00:13:16.127 --> 00:13:21.079
<v Tutor>Look for the cause: worsening shock, a rising lactate, or failing liver function.

185
00:13:21.079 --> 00:13:22.026
<v Tutor>Then act.

186
00:13:22.026 --> 00:13:25.309
<v Tutor>Reduce the citrate load by lowering the blood flow.

187
00:13:25.309 --> 00:13:29.705
<v Tutor>Increase the effluent or dialysate to clear more citrate in the filter.

188
00:13:29.705 --> 00:13:33.656
<v Tutor>If the ratio doesn't improve, stop citrate and change strategy.

189
00:13:33.656 --> 00:13:37.107
<v Tutor>Keep giving calcium until the ionised calcium is safe.

190
00:13:37.107 --> 00:13:44.452
<v Tutor>As the accumulated citrate is finally metabolised, all that bound calcium is released, and the bicarbonate load arrives too.

191
00:13:44.452 --> 00:13:48.292
<v Tutor>So expect a rebound later, with hypercalcaemia and alkalosis.

192
00:13:48.292 --> 00:13:51.520
<v Tutor>That rebound is an examiner's favourite follow-up.

193
00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:55.610
<v Tutor>Option four: prostacyclin, epoprostenol, or its analogues.

194
00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:59.523
<v Tutor>It inhibits platelet aggregation, with a half-life of minutes.

195
00:13:59.523 --> 00:14:03.213
<v Tutor>Advantages: less bleeding, and a short duration of action.

196
00:14:03.213 --> 00:14:12.437
<v Tutor>Disadvantages: vasodilatation and hypotension in a patient who may already be on noradrenaline, cost, and a filter life shorter than with citrate or heparin.

197
00:14:12.437 --> 00:14:15.120
<v Tutor>Again, it does not treat the thrombosis.

198
00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:18.346
<v Tutor>Option five: the direct thrombin inhibitors.

199
00:14:18.346 --> 00:14:23.888
<v Tutor>Argatroban, bivalirudin, and historically lepirudin and hirudin, which are no longer marketed.

200
00:14:23.888 --> 00:14:28.290
<v Tutor>They bind thrombin directly, so there is no antibody to cross-react with.

201
00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:32.800
<v Tutor>This is the class that treats the patient and the circuit at the same time.

202
00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:34.194
<v Tutor>Pause here.

203
00:14:34.194 --> 00:14:37.437
<v Tutor>You have a patient with HITTS and acute kidney injury.

204
00:14:37.437 --> 00:14:42.720
<v Tutor>Which direct thrombin inhibitor would you choose, and what laboratory artefact does it cause?

205
00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:44.080
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

206
00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:50.523
<v Tutor>Argatroban.

207
00:14:50.523 --> 00:14:54.859
<v Tutor>It is cleared by the liver, so it does not accumulate in renal failure.

208
00:14:54.859 --> 00:15:00.348
<v Tutor>That makes it the drug of choice in HIT with kidney injury, as long as the liver is working.

209
00:15:00.348 --> 00:15:02.324
<v Tutor>KDIGO 2012 says so directly.

210
00:15:02.324 --> 00:15:15.113
<v Tutor>In patients with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, all heparin must be stopped and direct thrombin inhibitors such as argatroban, or factor ten A inhibitors such as danaparoid or fondaparinux, are recommended, at grade one A.

211
00:15:15.113 --> 00:15:20.108
<v Tutor>In those without severe liver failure, argatroban is suggested ahead of the others.

212
00:15:20.108 --> 00:15:26.091
<v Tutor>The artefact: argatroban prolongs the prothrombin time and raises the international normalised ratio.

213
00:15:26.091 --> 00:15:31.964
<v Tutor>That matters when you switch to warfarin later, because the INR will overstate the warfarin effect.

214
00:15:31.964 --> 00:15:38.221
<v Tutor>Monitor with the activated partial thromboplastin time, aiming for one and a half to three times baseline.

215
00:15:38.221 --> 00:15:50.352
<v Tutor>Critically ill patients need much lower doses than the product information suggests, often starting around 0.5 to 1.2 micrograms per kilogram per minute, because hepatic clearance falls in shock and heart failure.

216
00:15:50.352 --> 00:15:56.280
<v Tutor>Disadvantages of the class: no reversal agent, cost, and accumulation of the renally cleared agents.

217
00:15:56.460 --> 00:16:02.974
<v Tutor>Bivalirudin is cleared partly by the kidney, so it accumulates in renal failure, but its short half-life makes it workable.

218
00:16:02.974 --> 00:16:06.604
<v Tutor>Lepirudin and hirudin were renally cleared and accumulated badly.

219
00:16:06.604 --> 00:16:14.460
<v Tutor>The College answer also notes that the relationship between hirudin level and the partial thromboplastin time is only roughly linear at the lower end.

220
00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:19.584
<v Tutor>Option six: danaparoid, a heparinoid that works mainly against factor ten A.

221
00:16:19.584 --> 00:16:23.184
<v Tutor>Advantages: it works in HIT and has a long track record.

222
00:16:23.184 --> 00:16:33.420
<v Tutor>Disadvantages: a long half-life, renal clearance, monitoring only by anti-ten-A levels, limited availability, and occasional cross-reactivity with heparin-induced antibodies.

223
00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:35.894
<v Tutor>Fondaparinux sits in the same space.

224
00:16:35.894 --> 00:16:43.880
<v Tutor>It has a long half-life and is renally cleared, so it accumulates in acute kidney injury, and the evidence for circuit use is limited.

225
00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:54.060
<v Tutor>The 2014 College answer lists it, along with nafamostat, a serine protease inhibitor used in Japan and Korea, which it describes as limited in experience and very expensive.

226
00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:56.706
<v Tutor>Now, how does Oh's frame this?

227
00:16:56.706 --> 00:17:03.503
<v Tutor>Chapter 56, Renal replacement therapy, covers circuit anticoagulation in the section on continuous therapy.

228
00:17:03.503 --> 00:17:08.999
<v Tutor>The heparin-induced thrombocytopenia material is in chapter 113, Haemostatic failure.

229
00:17:08.999 --> 00:17:13.491
<v Tutor>Put them together and you get the consultant answer to today's stem.

230
00:17:13.491 --> 00:17:15.973
<v Tutor>One: stop all heparin, everywhere.

231
00:17:15.973 --> 00:17:25.075
<v Tutor>Two: confirm HIT, with a pre-test probability using the four Ts score, then a platelet factor four immunoassay, then a functional assay if needed.

232
00:17:25.075 --> 00:17:32.876
<v Tutor>Three: start a systemic non-heparin anticoagulant, usually argatroban, because the patient has thrombosis and kidney injury.

233
00:17:32.876 --> 00:17:38.254
<v Tutor>Four: that same drug anticoagulates the circuit, so you rarely need citrate on top.

234
00:17:38.254 --> 00:17:44.105
<v Tutor>Five: optimise access, blood flow and pre-dilution to reduce how hard the drug has to work.

235
00:17:44.105 --> 00:17:51.020
<v Tutor>Six: don't give platelets for a low count alone, and don't start warfarin until the platelets have recovered.

236
00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:53.026
<v Tutor>What are the four Ts?

237
00:17:53.026 --> 00:17:58.288
<v Tutor>Thrombocytopenia: a fall of more than 50 percent, with a nadir that's usually above 20.

238
00:17:58.288 --> 00:18:04.824
<v Tutor>Timing: typically days 5 to 10 of heparin, or within a day if the patient was exposed in the previous 30 days.

239
00:18:04.824 --> 00:18:09.588
<v Tutor>Thrombosis: new venous or arterial clots, or skin necrosis at injection sites.

240
00:18:09.588 --> 00:18:19.225
<v Tutor>And other causes: in a septic patient on a filter there are many, which is why the score in the ICU often lands in the intermediate range and you need the laboratory.

241
00:18:19.225 --> 00:18:32.020
<v Tutor>The American Society of Hematology's 2018 guideline on heparin-induced thrombocytopenia sets this out, and recommends a non-heparin anticoagulant once the probability is intermediate or high, without waiting for the result.

242
00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:36.930
<v Tutor>A few management details from the same guideline earn marks in a HIT question.

243
00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:45.425
<v Tutor>Treat with a non-heparin anticoagulant at therapeutic dose, not prophylactic dose, because the thrombotic risk is high even if no clot has been found yet.

244
00:18:45.425 --> 00:18:52.190
<v Tutor>Look for occult deep vein thrombosis with a duplex ultrasound of the legs, including the arms if there are lines in them.

245
00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:59.374
<v Tutor>Do not give platelet transfusions just to correct the number, but they can be given for active bleeding or a high-risk procedure.

246
00:18:59.374 --> 00:19:01.943
<v Tutor>Do not start warfarin in the acute phase.

247
00:19:01.943 --> 00:19:08.183
<v Tutor>Warfarin drops protein C before it drops the procoagulant factors, so it can cause venous limb gangrene in HIT.

248
00:19:08.183 --> 00:19:11.959
<v Tutor>If warfarin has already been started, reverse it with vitamin K.

249
00:19:11.959 --> 00:19:17.937
<v Tutor>Start it only once platelets have recovered, usually above 150, and overlap it with the non-heparin agent.

250
00:19:17.937 --> 00:19:24.387
<v Tutor>The direct oral anticoagulants are an option once the patient is stable, but not with this degree of kidney injury.

251
00:19:24.387 --> 00:19:27.638
<v Tutor>Duration: around three months if there was thrombosis.

252
00:19:27.638 --> 00:19:32.620
<v Tutor>And document it as a heparin allergy, so no one restarts heparin in the next admission.

253
00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:35.752
<v Tutor>In the sepsis stem, there's one more twist.

254
00:19:35.752 --> 00:19:43.358
<v Tutor>The four Ts may not distinguish heparin-induced thrombocytopenia from sepsis, disseminated intravascular coagulation or haemodilution.

255
00:19:43.358 --> 00:19:46.839
<v Tutor>A filter that consumes platelets can lower the count too.

256
00:19:46.839 --> 00:19:49.089
<v Tutor>That's why the laboratory matters.

257
00:19:49.089 --> 00:19:54.713
<v Tutor>And while you wait for the result, an intermediate or high probability means you treat it as HIT.

258
00:19:54.713 --> 00:19:57.713
<v Tutor>The cost of missing it is limb loss or a stroke.

259
00:19:57.713 --> 00:20:00.980
<v Tutor>The cost of overtreating is a few days of argatroban.

260
00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:07.387
<v Tutor>A quick word on the guideline landscape, because examiners like a candidate who knows what is current.

261
00:20:07.387 --> 00:20:11.429
<v Tutor>KDIGO's 2012 acute kidney injury guideline is still the reference.

262
00:20:11.429 --> 00:20:19.020
<v Tutor>A 2026 update to it was released as a draft for public review this year, and the final version is not yet published, so quote 2012.

263
00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:23.007
<v Tutor>Its anticoagulation recommendations are worth learning by number.

264
00:20:23.007 --> 00:20:30.161
<v Tutor>For continuous therapy, regional citrate rather than heparin in patients without contraindications to citrate, grade two B.

265
00:20:30.161 --> 00:20:35.568
<v Tutor>If citrate is contraindicated, unfractionated or low molecular weight heparin, grade two C.

266
00:20:35.568 --> 00:20:40.265
<v Tutor>If bleeding risk is high, citrate rather than no anticoagulation, grade two C.

267
00:20:40.265 --> 00:20:46.217
<v Tutor>And the heparin-induced thrombocytopenia recommendation at grade one A, which I've already given you.

268
00:20:46.217 --> 00:20:56.922
<v Tutor>A Chinese Society of Critical Care Medicine guideline published in August 2026 also makes regional citrate first line, with heparin preferred where there is a risk of citrate accumulation.

269
00:20:56.922 --> 00:20:59.980
<v Tutor>That's the same message from a second direction.

270
00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:02.610
<v Tutor>Where do candidates lose marks?

271
00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:12.846
<v Tutor>From the College material on the 2014 question: candidates gave regional strategies alone, without recognising that HITTS still needs a systemic non-heparin anticoagulant.

272
00:21:12.846 --> 00:21:15.477
<v Tutor>The highest mark that sitting was 7.5.

273
00:21:15.477 --> 00:21:27.200
<v Tutor>And from your own work: yesterday, on the 2015 second sitting question comparing intermittent haemodialysis, slow low-efficiency dialysis and continuous haemofiltration, you scored five and a half.

274
00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:32.403
<v Tutor>The fix was to add the disadvantages, and the biggest omission was anticoagulation.

275
00:21:32.403 --> 00:21:38.922
<v Tutor>Continuous therapy needs continuous anticoagulation, with its bleeding risk and its effect on filter life.

276
00:21:38.922 --> 00:21:41.896
<v Tutor>Intermittent haemodialysis often needs none.

277
00:21:41.896 --> 00:21:45.327
<v Tutor>Slow low-efficiency dialysis gives shorter exposure.

278
00:21:45.327 --> 00:21:48.472
<v Tutor>Today's topic is the other half of that answer.

279
00:21:48.472 --> 00:21:55.220
<v Tutor>Once you've learned this, anticoagulation becomes a row in every renal replacement comparison table you write.

280
00:21:55.620 --> 00:21:57.914
<v Tutor>Three numbers to remember.

281
00:21:57.914 --> 00:22:03.988
<v Tutor>One: post-filter ionised calcium of 0.25 to 0.35 millimoles per litre for citrate.

282
00:22:03.988 --> 00:22:09.791
<v Tutor>Two: a total to ionised calcium ratio above 2.5 means citrate is accumulating.

283
00:22:09.791 --> 00:22:15.460
<v Tutor>Three: keep the filtration fraction under 20 to 25 percent in post-dilution.

284
00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:20.495
<v Tutor>Two trials changed circuit anticoagulation from habit to evidence.

285
00:22:20.495 --> 00:22:22.608
<v Tutor>Both compared citrate with heparin.

286
00:22:22.608 --> 00:22:27.275
<v Tutor>One was run in Australia and New Zealand, so an examiner here may quote it back at you.

287
00:22:27.275 --> 00:22:33.121
<v Tutor>Before we start, which outcome would you expect citrate to win on, and which would you expect it not to change?

288
00:22:33.121 --> 00:22:34.300
<v Tutor>Hold your guess.

289
00:22:34.700 --> 00:22:41.603
<v Tutor>The first is the Heparin Citrate Study, by David Gattas and colleagues, published in Critical Care Medicine in 2015.

290
00:22:41.603 --> 00:22:47.393
<v Tutor>It ran from 2010 to 2013 in seven intensive care units, six in Australia and one in New Zealand.

291
00:22:47.393 --> 00:22:52.293
<v Tutor>It randomised 212 adults with acute kidney injury who needed continuous therapy.

292
00:22:52.293 --> 00:23:01.757
<v Tutor>The comparison was regional citrate, with calcium replacement to keep systemic calcium normal, against regional heparin, reversed with protamine after the filter.

293
00:23:01.757 --> 00:23:04.986
<v Tutor>Note that this was regional heparin, not systemic.

294
00:23:04.986 --> 00:23:08.716
<v Tutor>Patients with liver failure or suspected HIT were excluded.

295
00:23:08.716 --> 00:23:12.780
<v Tutor>The primary outcome was the functional life of the first circuit.

296
00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:17.735
<v Tutor>Median life was 39.2 hours with citrate against 22.8 hours with regional heparin.

297
00:23:17.735 --> 00:23:21.910
<v Tutor>The hazard of filter clotting was about twice as high with heparin.

298
00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:27.144
<v Tutor>There was no difference in ICU mortality, 26.7 versus 23.4 percent, or in transfusion.

299
00:23:27.144 --> 00:23:33.657
<v Tutor>Adverse events were more common with heparin, mostly suspected or confirmed heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.

300
00:23:33.657 --> 00:23:36.330
<v Tutor>Criticisms: open label, as it had to be.

301
00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:39.280
<v Tutor>Underpowered for any patient-centred outcome.

302
00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:45.460
<v Tutor>Three quarters of patients came from two centres, and many potentially eligible patients were excluded.

303
00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:55.275
<v Tutor>What it changed: it gave Australian and New Zealand units local evidence that citrate keeps the circuit alive longer than the regional heparin and protamine technique.

304
00:23:55.275 --> 00:24:05.240
<v Tutor>One sentence for an exam answer: in the ANZ Heparin Citrate Study, regional citrate nearly doubled first-circuit life over regional heparin, with no difference in mortality.

305
00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:11.174
<v Tutor>The second is RICH, by Alexander Zarbock and colleagues, published in JAMA in 2020.

306
00:24:11.174 --> 00:24:15.491
<v Tutor>It was run in 26 German intensive care units from 2016 to 2018.

307
00:24:15.491 --> 00:24:24.613
<v Tutor>Adults with stage three acute kidney injury needing continuous therapy were randomised to regional citrate or systemic unfractionated heparin.

308
00:24:24.613 --> 00:24:30.694
<v Tutor>The citrate arm targeted a post-filter ionised calcium of 0.25 to 0.35 millimoles per litre.

309
00:24:30.694 --> 00:24:35.742
<v Tutor>The heparin arm targeted a partial thromboplastin time of 45 to 60 seconds.

310
00:24:35.742 --> 00:24:40.850
<v Tutor>Both arms received an effluent dose of 30 millilitres per kilogram per hour.

311
00:24:40.850 --> 00:24:45.350
<v Tutor>There were two primary outcomes, filter life and 90-day mortality.

312
00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:52.343
<v Tutor>The trial was stopped at an interim analysis after 638 patients were randomised, of whom 596 were analysed.

313
00:24:52.343 --> 00:24:58.120
<v Tutor>Filter life had crossed its efficacy boundary, and mortality had crossed into futility.

314
00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:05.086
<v Tutor>Before I give you the numbers: what was the median filter life with citrate in RICH, roughly, and with heparin?

315
00:25:05.086 --> 00:25:06.520
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

316
00:25:11.920 --> 00:25:17.870
<v Tutor>Median filter life was 47 hours with citrate and 26 hours with heparin, a difference of 15 hours.

317
00:25:17.870 --> 00:25:24.443
<v Tutor>Ninety-day mortality was 51.2 percent with citrate and 53.6 percent with heparin, which was not significant.

318
00:25:24.443 --> 00:25:29.600
<v Tutor>Bleeding complications were 5.1 percent with citrate and 16.9 percent with heparin.

319
00:25:29.600 --> 00:25:30.847
<v Tutor>Now the twist.

320
00:25:30.847 --> 00:25:36.173
<v Tutor>New culture-proven infections were more common with citrate, 68.0 versus 55.4 percent.

321
00:25:36.173 --> 00:25:43.710
<v Tutor>That was a secondary outcome among many, with no correction for multiple comparisons, so treat it as a signal, not a finding.

322
00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:49.377
<v Tutor>Citrate also caused more hypophosphataemia, and more severe alkalosis, though that was rare.

323
00:25:49.377 --> 00:25:53.797
<v Tutor>The criticisms: stopped early, so the mortality estimate is imprecise.

324
00:25:53.797 --> 00:25:54.873
<v Tutor>Open label.

325
00:25:54.873 --> 00:25:59.463
<v Tutor>About 6 percent of randomised patients were excluded for lack of consent.

326
00:25:59.463 --> 00:26:08.077
<v Tutor>And patients at highest risk of citrate accumulation, with severe liver failure or profound shock, were excluded, so it says nothing about them.

327
00:26:08.077 --> 00:26:13.177
<v Tutor>What it changed: it confirmed the 2012 KDIGO suggestion with a large modern trial.

328
00:26:13.177 --> 00:26:17.200
<v Tutor>Citrate is the default for continuous therapy where it is safe.

329
00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:28.660
<v Tutor>In an SAQ: the RICH trial showed regional citrate prolonged filter life compared with systemic heparin, 47 versus 26 hours, and reduced bleeding, without a demonstrated mortality benefit.

330
00:26:29.060 --> 00:26:30.805
<v Tutor>So, was your guess right?

331
00:26:30.805 --> 00:26:33.555
<v Tutor>Citrate wins on the circuit and on bleeding.

332
00:26:33.555 --> 00:26:36.147
<v Tutor>Neither trial showed it changes survival.

333
00:26:36.147 --> 00:26:43.710
<v Tutor>That is the honest position, and it's what an examiner wants: citrate is chosen for filter life and safety, not because it saves lives.

334
00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:47.570
<v Tutor>There's a nice irony in the history of continuous therapy itself.

335
00:26:47.570 --> 00:26:53.864
<v Tutor>In 1977, Peter Kramer in Göttingen connected a haemofilter between a patient's femoral artery and femoral vein.

336
00:26:53.864 --> 00:26:55.239
<v Tutor>There was no pump.

337
00:26:55.239 --> 00:27:03.278
<v Tutor>The patient's own blood pressure drove about 100 millilitres a minute through the filter, to treat fluid overload that diuretics couldn't shift.

338
00:27:03.278 --> 00:27:08.672
<v Tutor>That was continuous arteriovenous haemofiltration, the ancestor of every circuit in your unit.

339
00:27:08.672 --> 00:27:14.754
<v Tutor>A pumpless circuit at low flow clots easily, and anticoagulation was a problem from the very first patient.

340
00:27:14.754 --> 00:27:22.740
<v Tutor>Fifty years on, the question of how to keep blood moving through plastic without clotting the patient or the filter is still a core exam topic.

341
00:27:24.140 --> 00:27:24.953
<v Examiner>Right.

342
00:27:24.953 --> 00:27:28.261
<v Examiner>This is from the 2015 first sitting, question 22.

343
00:27:28.261 --> 00:27:31.917
<v Examiner>It is identical to the 2013 first sitting, question 29.

344
00:27:31.917 --> 00:27:37.604
<v Examiner>Regarding regional citrate anticoagulation for continuous renal replacement therapy, CRRT.

345
00:27:37.604 --> 00:27:38.475
<v Examiner>Part a.

346
00:27:38.475 --> 00:27:42.654
<v Examiner>What is the mechanism by which citrate provides anticoagulation?

347
00:27:42.654 --> 00:27:44.511
<v Examiner>Twenty percent of marks.

348
00:27:44.511 --> 00:27:45.381
<v Examiner>Part b.

349
00:27:45.381 --> 00:27:48.283
<v Examiner>What is the metabolic fate of the citrate?

350
00:27:48.283 --> 00:27:49.618
<v Examiner>Twenty percent.

351
00:27:49.618 --> 00:27:50.489
<v Examiner>Part c.

352
00:27:50.489 --> 00:27:53.390
<v Examiner>What are the features of citrate toxicity?

353
00:27:53.390 --> 00:27:54.725
<v Examiner>Twenty percent.

354
00:27:54.725 --> 00:27:55.596
<v Examiner>Part d.

355
00:27:55.596 --> 00:27:59.426
<v Examiner>What conditions may increase the risk of citrate toxicity?

356
00:27:59.426 --> 00:28:00.761
<v Examiner>Twenty percent.

357
00:28:00.761 --> 00:28:01.631
<v Examiner>Part e.

358
00:28:01.631 --> 00:28:07.203
<v Examiner>What alternative or alternatives to citrate could you use in a patient with severe HITS?

359
00:28:07.203 --> 00:28:08.538
<v Examiner>Twenty percent.

360
00:28:08.538 --> 00:28:10.917
<v Examiner>You have ten minutes in the exam.

361
00:28:10.917 --> 00:28:12.020
<v Examiner>Off you go.

362
00:28:12.520 --> 00:28:13.355
<v Candidate>Part a.

363
00:28:13.355 --> 00:28:15.638
<v Candidate>Citrate chelates ionised calcium.

364
00:28:15.638 --> 00:28:25.103
<v Candidate>Calcium is a cofactor at several steps of the coagulation cascade, including the activation of factors nine and ten and the conversion of prothrombin to thrombin.

365
00:28:25.103 --> 00:28:34.624
<v Candidate>Citrate is infused before the filter to bring the post-filter ionised calcium down to about 0.25 to 0.35 millimoles per litre, which stops clotting in the circuit.

366
00:28:34.624 --> 00:28:42.920
<v Candidate>Calcium is then infused into the patient, separately, to restore the systemic ionised calcium to normal, so the patient isn't anticoagulated.

367
00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:44.140
<v Examiner>Good.

368
00:28:44.140 --> 00:28:50.509
<v Examiner>That's full marks for a, provided you say the post-filter target and that calcium is replaced systemically.

369
00:28:50.509 --> 00:28:51.340
<v Examiner>Part b.

370
00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:52.622
<v Candidate>Part b.

371
00:28:52.622 --> 00:28:57.677
<v Candidate>A proportion of the calcium citrate complex is removed across the filter in the effluent.

372
00:28:57.677 --> 00:29:04.817
<v Candidate>The rest returns to the patient and is metabolised, mainly in the liver, with some in muscle and kidney, through the Krebs cycle.

373
00:29:04.817 --> 00:29:07.840
<v Candidate>The calcium is released back into the circulation.

374
00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:11.060
<v Examiner>And what does metabolising citrate produce?

375
00:29:11.560 --> 00:29:13.651
<v Candidate>Energy, through the Krebs cycle.

376
00:29:13.651 --> 00:29:15.324
<v Candidate>And, sorry, bicarbonate.

377
00:29:15.324 --> 00:29:18.200
<v Candidate>Each citrate molecule yields three bicarbonate.

378
00:29:18.700 --> 00:29:19.434
<v Examiner>Yes.

379
00:29:19.434 --> 00:29:23.655
<v Examiner>Three bicarbonate per citrate, and NADH from the Krebs cycle.

380
00:29:23.655 --> 00:29:27.142
<v Examiner>That's the link to part c, so don't leave it out.

381
00:29:27.142 --> 00:29:28.060
<v Examiner>Part c.

382
00:29:28.560 --> 00:29:29.460
<v Candidate>Part c.

383
00:29:29.460 --> 00:29:31.980
<v Candidate>Citrate toxicity has two patterns.

384
00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:36.660
<v Candidate>The first is low ionised calcium, from inadequate calcium replacement.

385
00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:44.700
<v Candidate>Features are perioral tingling, paraesthesia, carpopedal spasm, tetany, a prolonged QT interval, arrhythmias, and hypotension.

386
00:29:44.700 --> 00:29:53.280
<v Candidate>The second is metabolic alkalosis, because of the bicarbonate load, together with hypernatraemia, from the sodium in trisodium citrate.

387
00:29:53.780 --> 00:29:56.941
<v Examiner>You've described what happens when the liver metabolises citrate too well.

388
00:29:56.941 --> 00:29:59.100
<v Examiner>What happens when it can't metabolise it at all?

389
00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:01.429
<v Candidate>Then citrate accumulates.

390
00:30:01.429 --> 00:30:06.528
<v Candidate>You get a high anion gap metabolic acidosis, because citrate is an unmeasured anion.

391
00:30:06.528 --> 00:30:15.230
<v Candidate>The ionised calcium falls, because citrate keeps binding it, but the total calcium rises, because you keep infusing calcium and it's all still bound.

392
00:30:15.230 --> 00:30:18.279
<v Candidate>So the ratio of total to ionised calcium rises.

393
00:30:18.279 --> 00:30:20.496
<v Candidate>Above about 2.5 is accumulation.

394
00:30:20.496 --> 00:30:23.600
<v Candidate>The calcium infusion requirement keeps climbing.

395
00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:32.960
<v Examiner>That's the answer the College wanted: two distinct patterns, alkalosis when metabolism is intact and acidosis with a high ratio when it is not.

396
00:30:32.960 --> 00:30:36.070
<v Examiner>You didn't volunteer the ratio until I asked.

397
00:30:36.070 --> 00:30:38.300
<v Examiner>In the real thing I can't ask.

398
00:30:38.300 --> 00:30:39.180
<v Examiner>Part d.

399
00:30:39.680 --> 00:30:40.528
<v Candidate>Part d.

400
00:30:40.528 --> 00:30:44.426
<v Candidate>Liver failure, because the liver does most of the metabolism.

401
00:30:44.426 --> 00:30:48.155
<v Candidate>Low cardiac output and shock, with poor hepatic perfusion.

402
00:30:48.155 --> 00:30:51.037
<v Candidate>Severe lactic acidosis is a marker of that.

403
00:30:51.037 --> 00:30:53.240
<v Candidate>And pre-existing hypocalcaemia.

404
00:30:53.740 --> 00:30:54.397
<v Examiner>Fine.

405
00:30:54.397 --> 00:31:01.022
<v Examiner>You could add that a high blood flow means more citrate delivered, and that a massive transfusion is itself a citrate load.

406
00:31:01.022 --> 00:31:01.780
<v Examiner>Part e.

407
00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:03.038
<v Candidate>Part e.

408
00:31:03.038 --> 00:31:05.314
<v Candidate>In severe HITS, I'd stop all heparin.

409
00:31:05.314 --> 00:31:12.140
<v Candidate>Alternatives for the circuit are prostacyclin, which is a platelet inhibitor with a short half-life, but it causes hypotension.

410
00:31:12.140 --> 00:31:15.680
<v Candidate>Or no anticoagulation with a high blood flow and pre-dilution.

411
00:31:16.180 --> 00:31:17.129
<v Examiner>Stop there.

412
00:31:17.129 --> 00:31:20.924
<v Examiner>This patient has severe heparin-induced thrombotic thrombocytopenia.

413
00:31:20.924 --> 00:31:24.020
<v Examiner>What does the patient need, regardless of the circuit?

414
00:31:24.520 --> 00:31:26.384
<v Candidate>Systemic anticoagulation.

415
00:31:26.384 --> 00:31:28.249
<v Candidate>With a non-heparin agent.

416
00:31:28.249 --> 00:31:32.938
<v Candidate>So, argatroban, which is hepatically cleared and suitable in renal failure.

417
00:31:32.938 --> 00:31:37.401
<v Candidate>Bivalirudin, which has a short half-life but is partly renally cleared.

418
00:31:37.401 --> 00:31:42.768
<v Candidate>Danaparoid, which has anti-ten-A activity, a long half-life, and some cross-reactivity.

419
00:31:42.768 --> 00:31:46.327
<v Candidate>Fondaparinux, which is renally cleared and accumulates.

420
00:31:46.327 --> 00:31:52.880
<v Candidate>And the argatroban infusion anticoagulates the circuit as well, so I wouldn't need a regional method on top.

421
00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:55.461
<v Examiner>That's what gets the marks.

422
00:31:55.461 --> 00:32:05.507
<v Examiner>The College answer wanted at least three classes or four named agents for full marks: prostacyclin, argatroban, danaparoid, bivalirudin, fondaparinux, lepirudin.

423
00:32:05.507 --> 00:32:13.888
<v Examiner>And the reasoning that the patient, not just the filter, needs a non-heparin anticoagulant is what separates a pass from a good pass.

424
00:32:13.888 --> 00:32:19.060
<v Examiner>Your first answer to e would have earned about two of the five marks it needed.

425
00:32:19.460 --> 00:32:21.723
<v Examiner>Let me mark the whole thing.

426
00:32:21.723 --> 00:32:23.421
<v Examiner>Part a, full marks.

427
00:32:23.421 --> 00:32:28.765
<v Examiner>Part b, nearly full, but only once I prompted you for the three bicarbonates.

428
00:32:28.765 --> 00:32:35.618
<v Examiner>Part c, about two thirds without prompting, because the accumulation pattern and the ratio came late.

429
00:32:35.618 --> 00:32:36.938
<v Examiner>Part d, full.

430
00:32:36.938 --> 00:32:39.704
<v Examiner>Part e, about half as first written.

431
00:32:39.704 --> 00:32:42.219
<v Examiner>Overall, about seven out of ten.

432
00:32:42.219 --> 00:32:48.254
<v Examiner>In the sitting, the pass rate on this question was 86 percent, and the top mark was 9.3.

433
00:32:48.254 --> 00:32:51.209
<v Examiner>So seven is a pass on an easy question.

434
00:32:51.209 --> 00:32:54.227
<v Examiner>On an easy question, seven isn't enough.

435
00:32:54.227 --> 00:33:08.876
<v Examiner>The one fix: for every citrate answer, write two columns for toxicity, the liver metabolising it, giving alkalosis and high sodium, and the liver failing, giving acidosis and a rising total to ionised calcium ratio above 2.5.

436
00:33:08.876 --> 00:33:16.860
<v Examiner>And whenever a stem says HITTS, write in your first line: stop all heparin, start a systemic non-heparin anticoagulant.

437
00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:23.555
<v Examiner>A follow-up, the kind you'd get in a viva, and the kind that turns up as a data question.

438
00:33:23.555 --> 00:33:25.437
<v Examiner>Day three on citrate.

439
00:33:25.437 --> 00:33:28.033
<v Examiner>Sodium 149 millimoles per litre.

440
00:33:28.033 --> 00:33:30.888
<v Examiner>Bicarbonate 34 millimoles per litre.

441
00:33:30.888 --> 00:33:31.927
<v Examiner>pH 7.52.

442
00:33:31.927 --> 00:33:37.702
<v Examiner>Ionised calcium 1.1 millimoles per litre, total calcium 2.3 millimoles per litre.

443
00:33:37.702 --> 00:33:39.260
<v Examiner>What's going on?

444
00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:43.040
<v Candidate>The ratio is about 2.1, so it isn't accumulating.

445
00:33:43.040 --> 00:33:49.371
<v Candidate>The liver is metabolising the citrate well, and the bicarbonate load is causing a metabolic alkalosis.

446
00:33:49.371 --> 00:33:52.364
<v Candidate>The sodium is up from the trisodium citrate.

447
00:33:52.364 --> 00:34:02.723
<v Candidate>I'd reduce the blood flow to deliver less citrate, increase the dialysate flow to clear more of it, and check whether there's a lower-bicarbonate dialysate in the protocol.

448
00:34:02.723 --> 00:34:07.960
<v Candidate>I'd also check for other causes of alkalosis, like nasogastric losses or diuretics.

449
00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:09.084
<v Examiner>Good.

450
00:34:09.084 --> 00:34:13.688
<v Examiner>That's the commonest citrate problem in a well patient, and the reverse of accumulation.

451
00:34:13.688 --> 00:34:18.820
<v Examiner>A candidate who can tell those two patterns apart from a single blood gas has understood the topic.

452
00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:20.726
<v Candidate>Can I ask one thing?

453
00:34:20.726 --> 00:34:24.240
<v Candidate>If the circuit keeps clotting on argatroban, do I add citrate?

454
00:34:24.740 --> 00:34:26.547
<v Examiner>You can, and some units do.

455
00:34:26.547 --> 00:34:31.917
<v Examiner>But first check the access and the flows, and check that the argatroban is actually therapeutic.

456
00:34:31.917 --> 00:34:37.596
<v Examiner>A partial thromboplastin time drawn from a line with saline flush running is a common source of error.

457
00:34:37.596 --> 00:34:39.920
<v Examiner>Then raise the dose within the range.

458
00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:42.140
<v Examiner>Most of the time it's the catheter.

459
00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:49.416
<v Tutor>Let me pull the exam practice together, because the College could easily combine these two stems.

460
00:34:49.416 --> 00:34:53.484
<v Tutor>If you see HITTS on a filter, your answer has three layers.

461
00:34:53.484 --> 00:35:03.382
<v Tutor>First: stop every source of heparin, including flushes, locks and coated membranes, and send the four Ts, the immunoassay and if needed a functional assay.

462
00:35:03.382 --> 00:35:12.248
<v Tutor>Second: the systemic non-heparin anticoagulant, normally argatroban in kidney injury, with its dose, its monitoring, and the INR artefact.

463
00:35:12.248 --> 00:35:15.284
<v Tutor>Third, as a table: the circuit strategies.

464
00:35:15.284 --> 00:35:17.956
<v Tutor>Access, blood flow and pre-dilution.

465
00:35:17.956 --> 00:35:19.231
<v Tutor>Prostacyclin.

466
00:35:19.231 --> 00:35:21.599
<v Tutor>Regional citrate as an adjunct.

467
00:35:21.599 --> 00:35:24.636
<v Tutor>Each with an advantage and a disadvantage.

468
00:35:24.636 --> 00:35:30.040
<v Tutor>That covers the 2014, 2010 and 2007 versions, and part e of the citrate question.

469
00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:33.043
<v Tutor>One last question, from yesterday's answer.

470
00:35:33.043 --> 00:35:40.393
<v Tutor>Comparing continuous haemofiltration with intermittent haemodialysis, what anticoagulation disadvantage should be in the continuous row?

471
00:35:40.393 --> 00:35:41.720
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

472
00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:54.178
<v Tutor>Continuous therapy needs continuous anticoagulation, with ongoing bleeding risk, and clotting interrupts the delivered dose.

473
00:35:54.178 --> 00:35:58.508
<v Tutor>Intermittent haemodialysis often runs without any anticoagulation at all.

474
00:35:58.508 --> 00:36:03.160
<v Tutor>That row was worth a mark you didn't get yesterday, and it's yours from now on.

475
00:36:03.560 --> 00:36:06.385
<v Tutor>Before the next episode, do one thing.

476
00:36:06.385 --> 00:36:18.297
<v Tutor>Draw the HITTS circuit table from memory: strategy, advantage, disadvantage, six rows, with the stop-all-heparin line at the top and argatroban with its dose and INR artefact underneath.

477
00:36:18.297 --> 00:36:24.806
<v Tutor>Then check it against the College answer for the 2014 first sitting, question 29, on today's list.

478
00:36:24.806 --> 00:36:26.648
<v Tutor>Ten minutes, on paper.

479
00:36:26.648 --> 00:36:28.920
<v Tutor>This has been Part Two Daily.
